#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161793 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162001 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6553801-8b7e-5584-940d-a85600b44ec6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Using a Graph: One Mode"}] :question/id #uuid "21160b89-bb32-5dc5-9a4a-f7589905c913" :question/math-academy-id "e-8241" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function $f(x)$ shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n![](images/13/137e06758f63232479a573ffd7c7c68cffd832d8fac22036bce04e9968ad7c4a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that $f(x)$ has a single global maximum at $x=7.$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $7.$\n\n![](images/d5/d500063d0885dc2437d83754d8e54d81740a8b6fd1855416e51dab2577cb27a6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161794 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162002 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "469819e4-0596-5aba-944a-52609afc8af7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Using a Graph: Multiple Modes"}] :question/id #uuid "32f1849f-60e5-58bd-823a-f6bbbdb51153" :question/math-academy-id "e-8242" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n![](images/d6/d63a97b931fedb6b001f590134b448dd2222f33606725343486064cec5e85036.png)" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that the global maxima of $f(x)$ are at $x=1,7,13.$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $1,7,$ and $13.$\n\n![](images/f4/f4efdc7b16747f33e48be7c73369e4f881177d6faba19d02bc832bfa0e916278.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161795 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162003 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12f0497c-3359-5aeb-83d7-930ba3d1ab0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Algebraically: Multiple Modes"}] :question/id #uuid "e1167d53-7cfb-5ec2-96df-250e226d60b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8243" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n![](images/9e/9ec101ca3c5052991372544517cd3d497b6d58f6df7b81084d2fd16a9c74ac89.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ is $f(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}(x-1)^2.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 2(x-1) \\\\  & =3(x-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x) = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\n3(x-1)= 0\\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=1.\n$$\n\nThis critical point is within the interval $[0,2],$ so we keep it.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x = 0, 2.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,1, 2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{3}{2}(0-1)^{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{3}{2}(1-1)^{2}=0 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{3}{2}(2-1)^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(0 ) = f( 2) = \\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $0$ and $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161796 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162004 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e51181c-2074-5d7d-a9b8-d6f6128bc44c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Algebraically: One Mode"}] :question/id #uuid "8be540db-a27c-537c-a804-35ed7cc45168" :question/math-academy-id "e-8244" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n![](images/a3/a3de8797a3b489ada861edbebb5de935dc5c840dafc509bf2b41ad093d7cc2b4.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0 \\leq x\\leq \\pi$ is $f(x) = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{2}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x) = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos x}{2} = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}+k\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is any integer.\nAmong these critical points, only $x= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ lies within the interval $\\left[0, \\pi \\right].$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x = 0, \\pi.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0 \\\\ f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162004 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e51181c-2074-5d7d-a9b8-d6f6128bc44c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Algebraically: One Mode"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0127daaf-87d8-5cdd-bdc6-723f2bd9ef53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481773} {:answer/id #uuid "5aec8f18-e1a4-58de-8c20-9808604c0760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481987} {:answer/id #uuid "a109c617-97d0-5f35-b138-970c2477e232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482157} {:answer/id #uuid "ce66b1bb-2965-5a68-ba8d-76a5055610ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482263} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8f3418-48fb-5e2d-982c-77fd5497ed14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a109c617-97d0-5f35-b138-970c2477e232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482157} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf31510e-14a1-5ed8-a2ac-a70c7049efd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5361d7e0-4877-5c07-8e5e-b4d8b7ac0426" :question/math-academy-id "q-126179" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 3 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 3$ is $f(x)=\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{9}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{9}x=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nThe point $x=0$ is in the interval $[0,3],$ so we keep it.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,3.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot {0}^{2}=0 \\\\ f(3) & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot {3}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf(3)=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162001 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6553801-8b7e-5584-940d-a85600b44ec6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Using a Graph: One Mode"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ace53f9-ef9e-54a2-9c7a-c1056178fee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482018} {:answer/id #uuid "72df434c-41c5-5294-90c5-50f47b476fbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482041} {:answer/id #uuid "cfbbb1c3-17aa-5b82-a30a-4d5a5f5930fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482268} {:answer/id #uuid "d0116419-67e0-568e-8b47-799ed8f43f4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482271} {:answer/id #uuid "f11849a0-86b5-5c70-8410-6318e0b9033e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #