ions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162002 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "469819e4-0596-5aba-944a-52609afc8af7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Using a Graph: Multiple Modes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04657e13-e69b-5894-ac2e-427c99373af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5,7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481780} {:answer/id #uuid "21f6d952-ec75-52e8-8858-10b25f61865c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4,6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481866} {:answer/id #uuid "67afab5f-20f7-541f-84cf-2494975f72ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482014} {:answer/id #uuid "6f13b64d-2dce-53bf-8ff3-5482b2db4c08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2,5,7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482032} {:answer/id #uuid "bc717a72-c626-5254-aaa8-7c5effd6a89b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2,5,9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04657e13-e69b-5894-ac2e-427c99373af7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5,7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481780} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d875bb2-b179-54eb-ac35-d4ab00c3557d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed1f11b0-312f-5ca9-81fd-de20b37867fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-126294" :question/problem "![](images/f6/f6f2ce8adb8c1b35c2fd4928805f486920228b913c3a7fc18ddc60b26a8e8fcd.png)\n\nLet $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown above. What are the modes of $X?$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that the global maxima of $f(x)$ are at $x=5,7.$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $5$ and $7.$\n\n![](images/59/59b72efa72dd48bc2fb74fed5f795341f59af377af0756965abab9d23390d468.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162004 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e51181c-2074-5d7d-a9b8-d6f6128bc44c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Algebraically: One Mode"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1082a3b2-06e8-5365-82b5-32a2d22cab5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481815} {:answer/id #uuid "171293e6-ca8b-5e7f-9ecc-891ec0c09d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481840} {:answer/id #uuid "7442236d-8264-5a32-a3e2-b465b840d103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482045} {:answer/id #uuid "ca5d22cd-920b-5434-88ab-9c1bd372a226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482256} {:answer/id #uuid "d844503e-26ef-5f85-b233-34d70948d178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d844503e-26ef-5f85-b233-34d70948d178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482288} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d51fc38-d3aa-53d3-aca8-4f3a38a9dad3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90f3bc1d-94f7-5716-a7c9-9b34d22c275c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126323" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-3x},\\, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\, & x<0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $x\\ge 0$ is $f(x)=3{e}^{-3x}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-9{e}^{-3x}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are no solutions  of  ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoint $x=0.$ So, this is the only critical point.\n\nSince ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for $x>0,$ then $f(0)$ is the global maximum of $f.$ Therefore, the mode of $X$ is $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482588 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162004 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e51181c-2074-5d7d-a9b8-d6f6128bc44c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Algebraically: One Mode"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "156d8fb9-a3ef-5cf0-a116-cde0d34d5ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481835} {:answer/id #uuid "60d2f142-9346-53de-b0c8-157b88f8834d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481996} {:answer/id #uuid "961db6b5-e10b-5c17-9882-c4b693893797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482129} {:answer/id #uuid "b10db118-036b-5d52-8ae6-0ee7ed64170f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482191} {:answer/id #uuid "ce7a1336-f3e9-5ebe-b7db-72ffe18ae7da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce7a1336-f3e9-5ebe-b7db-72ffe18ae7da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482264} :answer-field/id #uuid "20ca85d7-4841-50ea-a46d-18e2741e2de1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c069136c-9e24-5901-bcc3-c8fd76fb24b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-126721" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}},\\, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $x\\ge 0$ is $f(x)=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{(1+x)^{3}}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are no solutions of  ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoint $x=0.$ So, this is the only critical point.\n\nSince ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for $x>0,$ then $f(0)$ is the global maximum of $f(x).$\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162003 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12f0497c-3359-5aeb-83d7-930ba3d1ab0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Algebraically: Multiple Modes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ac1ac8-5d12-5b50-934c-de82720f574b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481839} {:answer/id #uuid "1a3281f8-9551-52d3-8d0c-c6b89b2d2548" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481844} {:answer/id #uuid "22e163e2-c51b-5f8c-acce-093bdea2aa79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481868} {:answer/id #uuid "4dde1e24-43fb-5f71-b240-852e369c1dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,0,1,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481963} {:answer/id #uuid "c0cdcb5b-73ea-5836-bf70-b1a7b83f23d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a3281f8-9551-52d3-8d0c-c6b89b2d2548" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481844} :answer-field/id #uuid "68323756-6915-5a2a-b83f-b1d14e9cdd18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fed1e82d-9029-565f-bbd7-a48e9974d84e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127090" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{96}{595}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-2), & -1\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $-1\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2}$ is $f(x)=-\\frac{96}{595}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-2).$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{96}{595}(4{x}^{3}-4x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{384}{595}x({x}^{2}-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{384}{595}x({x}^{2}-1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[-1,\\frac{3}{2}],$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=\\pm 1.$ So, these are critical points as well, but $-1$ is included above.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,\\pm 1,\\frac{3}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-\\frac{96}{595}((-1)^{4}-2(-1)^{2}-2)=\\frac{288}{595} \\\\ f(0) & =-\\frac{96}{595}((0)^{4}-2(0)^{2}-2)=\\frac{192}{595} \\\\ f(1) & =-\\frac{96}{595}((1)^{4}-2(1)^{2}-2)=\\frac{288}{595} \\\\ f(\\frac{3}{2}) & =-\\frac{96}{595}({(\\frac{3}{2})}^{4}-2{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-2)=\\frac{138}{595}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(-1)=f(1)=\\frac{288}{595}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $-1$ and $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162651 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162003 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12f0497c-3359-5aeb-83d7-930ba3d1ab0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Algebraically: Multiple Modes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "115e77fb-e02b-5237-b31f-12a56efafa90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2,0,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481819} {:answer/id #uuid "71db68f4-8250-546a-b7d2-c4932f56d175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482040} {:answer/id #uuid "a4af405c-2d3b-5a9c-a6cd-6a91a01e3368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2},0,\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482165} {:answer/id #uuid "ee932987-4d59-5b7f-8b0d-6b1eb79047d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482344} {:answer/id #uuid "fb7e527d-fac9-5aad-b55d-08c430d951b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71db68f4-8250-546a-b7d2-c4932f56d175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482040} :answer-field/id #uuid "d05b0e67-0633-544d-b4ab-dfd417417c77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161198}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "df132e5e-2e4d-5e3b-bfb1-3123d68290f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94958" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15}{128}(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}), & -2\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $-2\\le x\\le 2$ is $f(x)=\\frac{15}{128}(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}).$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{15}{128}(8x-4{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{32}(2x-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{32}x(2-{x}^{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{32}x(2-{x}^{2})=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[-2,2],$ so we consider them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=\\pm 2.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,\\pm \\sqrt{2},\\pm 2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =0 \\\\ f(-\\sqrt{2}) & =\\frac{15}{128}(4{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}-{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{4})=\\frac{15}{128}(8-4)=\\frac{15}{32} \\\\ f(0) & =\\frac{15}{128}(4{(0)}^{2}-{(0)}^{4})=0 \\\\ f(\\sqrt{2}) & =\\frac{15}{128}(4{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{2})}^{4})=\\frac{15}{128}(8-4)=\\frac{15}{32} \\\\ f(2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(-\\sqrt{2})=f(\\sqrt{2})=\\frac{15}{32}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $-\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162001 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6553801-8b7e-5584-940d-a85600b44ec6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Using a Graph: One Mode"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d81d2ab-531d-549d-b5ac-689d2b4c45ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481804} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3eef74-994c-597e-9a30-d44b9d54cd76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482059} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e59895-d646-5997-ab1b-7089034ad825" :answer/type {:db/i