ble Algebraically: One Mode"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "393f65ce-23ad-5614-aa05-25d2d89acbb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481916} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc505f2-78f1-5c70-88f4-d6032b9a5c09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481927} {:answer/id #uuid "454ccc01-cdb3-5155-8a6e-65477458f869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481946} {:answer/id #uuid "b01250a0-7f57-5f95-bfbf-f046438b94d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482190} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd25a84-48d7-5c69-a712-cc443b6851fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "454ccc01-cdb3-5155-8a6e-65477458f869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481946} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f670097-2378-5e08-98c6-e7db12d192b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72c83079-fa51-5573-830e-81ba6d3220b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-95328" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 2$ is $f(x)=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are no solutions  of  ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,2.$ So, these are critical points.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{0}{2}=0 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{2}{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf(2)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482596 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162004 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e51181c-2074-5d7d-a9b8-d6f6128bc44c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Algebraically: One Mode"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eb7b064-6ea7-5770-979d-8abdcb24a1aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481810} {:answer/id #uuid "20ec2c09-e6fa-5afa-a648-fb02946dddda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481863} {:answer/id #uuid "58950bbd-e3ee-5c07-b30b-ddb473d62ab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481982} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f18087-3589-5bb1-b55b-ec1c1e75de92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482205} {:answer/id #uuid "c74131b8-caab-5a3e-a0d9-32a36917054f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5f18087-3589-5bb1-b55b-ec1c1e75de92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482205} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf38b3b7-889a-5d4b-add6-1de81acf50be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0fd2e28-4af0-53c5-9c68-348af97ab34c" :question/math-academy-id "q-95348" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{100}(5+4x-{x}^{2}), & 0\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 5$ is $f(x)=\\frac{3}{100}(5+4x-{x}^{2}).$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{3}{100}(4-2x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{100}(4-2x)=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nThe point $x=2$ is in the interval $[0,5],$ so we keep it.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,5.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,2,5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{3}{100}(5+4\\cdot 0-{0}^{2})=\\frac{15}{100}=\\frac{3}{20} \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{3}{100}(5+4\\cdot 2-{2}^{2})=\\frac{27}{100} \\\\ f(5) & =\\frac{3}{100}(5+4\\cdot 5-{5}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf(2)=\\frac{27}{100}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162003 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12f0497c-3359-5aeb-83d7-930ba3d1ab0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Algebraically: Multiple Modes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5650a8d9-5536-5945-b509-63800912cc45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481980} {:answer/id #uuid "5eefcdf0-d372-5d82-907e-206da45b0d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481994} {:answer/id #uuid "7fb401a5-847f-54af-abdb-c557eab69963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482067} {:answer/id #uuid "88c4e86f-2e4e-50ce-94cf-69673f2e804b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482091} {:answer/id #uuid "aec77f18-46f9-56bc-9c5f-171f74af8827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,0,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5650a8d9-5536-5945-b509-63800912cc45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481980} :answer-field/id #uuid "0846b1e1-5974-53e1-a637-306e6e3e8f94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cc6f0c6d-fbfc-515f-9faf-cdb2e36bd29a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95380" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{2}, & -1\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $-1\\le x\\le 1$ is $f(x)=\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{2}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =10{x}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n10{x}^{3}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nThis critical point is within the interval $[-1,1],$ so we keep it.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=-1,1.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=-1,0,1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =\\frac{5\\cdot (-1)^{4}}{2}=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ f(0) & =\\frac{5\\cdot {0}^{4}}{2}=0 \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{5\\cdot {1}^{4}}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(-1)=f(1)=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $-1$ and $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162003 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12f0497c-3359-5aeb-83d7-930ba3d1ab0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Algebraically: Multiple Modes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25cf873b-c561-529e-a150-dbd67081b794" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2,6,8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481873} {:answer/id #uuid "2a39c9ca-304c-5dc6-bf0a-a3191df9d199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,2,6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481882} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc1d53b-065c-5f28-baee-103cc2d04fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0,2,8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481893} {:answer/id #uuid "6c32fa51-116b-5e6b-8ad8-9c15a7a0762b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2,6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482023} {:answer/id #uuid "c956b69f-1371-5064-85a7-92f548f1708c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2,8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c956b69f-1371-5064-85a7-92f548f1708c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2,8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482253} :answer-field/id #uuid "f864eeb7-5c3a-520c-b5c5-87f43508e40a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "04da65b8-370f-5d43-9d9a-986d048126c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-97023" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x(6-x)^{2}}{128}, & 0\\le x\\le 8 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 8$ is $f(x)=\\frac{x(6-x)^{2}}{128}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(6-x)^{2}-2x(6-x)}{128} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6-x)(6-x-2x)}{128} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(6-x)(2-x)}{128},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3(6-x)(2-x)}{128}\\,⟹\\,x=2,6.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[0,8],$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,8.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,2,6,8:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{0(6-0)^{2}}{128}=0 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{2(6-2)^{2}}{128}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ f(6) & =\\frac{6(6-6)^{2}}{128}=0 \\\\ f(8) & =\\frac{8(6-8)^{2}}{128}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(2)=f(8)=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $2$ and $8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162003 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12f0497c-3359-5aeb-83d7-930ba3d1ab0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable Algebraically: Multiple Modes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "616aa6a5-4b43-534d-821a-bbdbc35c16f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481997} {:answer/id #uuid "8002f0e0-b8eb-52ab-98a1-fb52f05547d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1,1,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482069} {:answer/id #uuid "803b79a6-bd27-5019-a0ca-6c717c883e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482071} {:answer/id #uuid "91229bac-46a3-5f6c-aba9-119cb3512ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482116} {:answer/id #uuid "d675c4d0-7a79-5251-b122-3bcefc69e692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2,-1,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "803b79a6-bd27-5019-a0ca-6c717c883e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482071} :answer-field/id #uuid "89711e9c-e116-50a7-ae9d-6fbf9295b7f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3564d8be-ba15-5300-bf05-043e2a7c4f77" :question/math-academy-id "q-97025" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(2-x)(x+1)^{2}}{8}, & -2\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $-2\\le x\\le 2$ is $f(x)=\\frac{(2-x)(x+1)^{2}}{8}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{-(x+1)^{2}+2(2-x)(x+1)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(-x-1+4-2x)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(x+1)(1-x)}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3(x+1)(1-x)}{8}\\,⟹\\,x=-1,1.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[-2,2],$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=-2,2.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=-2,-1,1,2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =\\frac{(2+2)(-2+1)^{2}}{8}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(-1) & =\\frac{(2+1)(-1+1)^{2}}{8}=0 \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{(2-1)(1+1)^{2}}{8}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{(2-2)(2+1)^{2}}{8}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(-2)=f(1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $-2$ and $1.$"}]}
